 using that voice  now  and here's hoping that other young  quarterbacks in the nfl  don't just sit back and say patrick  you've got this  because i think just like with the  ravens as more teams say  hey yeah we agree with the ravens and  here's  our statement and it's the same as what  the ravens are saying  hopefully mahomes inspires other young  players especially quarterbacks because  they're the leaders on the field  to follow him and not just say go ahead  patrick  but to say we're right with you shoulder  to shoulder  yeah and i think it needs to be a lot of  the white quarterbacks and we saw it  with ryan tannehill  uh yesterday and a handful of others who  stood up there and  and stood with them and said the right  things and so yeah  it's got to be the quarterbacks because  they're the guys they're the faces  there's who they are who we  talk about so they need to be the ones  leading this charge and mahomes is the  leader of the leaders  but those other quarterbacks need to  step up to and and and come to the  forefront and be  big in this and look it's not just the  teams it's not  just the players the league office needs  to be part of this as well  and troy vincent the executive vp of  football operations was on espn radio  yesterday and  he was emotional his comments were  powerful and it  brings back a point that we made just  about at this same moment yesterday  that it's not necessarily concern about  what happens  to you it's concerned about what happens  to your children  because your children are  eventually turned loose into the world  and if you are a parent  of black children and you have to have  that  fear every time they leave your sight  every time they pull away in a car  and you don't know what's going to  happen to them if there's an interaction  with police  that is terrifying it's horrifying and i  think that  is for many of the adult males in the  sports world  who have children who are black that is  the concern  and it's it is if